    <h1>48 Hours in Monterey</h1>

    <h2>
      Two days of California history, Cannery Row, sea otters, and a bay that
      has shaped Monterey for centuries
    </h2>

    <p>
      Monterey is easy to misunderstand.
    </p>

    <p>
      Arrive for a few hours and you might see Cannery Row, visit the Aquarium,
      eat something near the waterfront, and conclude that you’ve seen the city.
    </p>

    <p>
      You haven’t.
    </p>

    <p>
      Long before Cannery Row became a visitor destination, Monterey was one of
      the most important settlements in early California. It served as
      California’s capital during the Spanish and Mexican periods, while ships
      entered its harbor carrying goods and news from distant ports.
    </p>

    <p>
      Later, enormous schools of sardines helped create an industrial waterfront
      lined with canneries.
    </p>

    <p>
      Then the sardines largely disappeared. The canneries closed. And Monterey
      reinvented itself again.
    </p>

    <p>
      Today, the same bay that supported those industries is internationally
      associated with marine research, conservation, recreation, and one of the
      world’s best-known aquariums.
    </p>

    <p>
      That’s the Monterey we’re going to explore.
    </p>

    <p>
      Not simply a town beside the Pacific, but a community whose story has
      repeatedly been shaped by it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>In Monterey, the ocean isn’t simply the view. It’s part of the story.</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>At a Glance</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Best for:</strong> Couples, families, history travelers, wildlife
      lovers, walkers, food travelers, and anyone interested in California’s
      relationship with the Pacific.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The pace:</strong> Easy and mostly walkable, with one substantial
      Aquarium visit and plenty of time beside the bay.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Stay:</strong> Two nights.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Don’t miss:</strong> Old Monterey, the waterfront, Cannery Row,
      the Monterey Bay Aquarium, and enough unscheduled time to simply watch
      the bay.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Good to know:</strong> Much of this itinerary can be experienced
      on foot. Once you’ve parked, leave the car alone whenever practical.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>
      Monterey gives us something many California coastal destinations don’t:
      the opportunity to park the car and forget about it for significant
      portions of the weekend.
    </p>

    <p>
      Look for lodging around <strong>downtown/Old Monterey, the waterfront,
      or Cannery Row</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each creates a slightly different experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      Downtown puts historic Monterey and restaurants within easy reach.
      Cannery Row places the Aquarium and bay outside your door. The waterfront
      gives you convenient access to Fisherman’s Wharf and the coastal
      recreation trail.
    </p>

    <p>
      For only two nights, favor location over saving a modest amount on a room
      farther away.
    </p>

    <p>
      Walking back to your hotel after dinner is part of the pleasure here.
    </p>

